///1HELP
Help by Willem Aandewiel
HELP.COM 3.3 - CP/M Help System

HELP displays documentation from .HLP files on your CP/M disk.
You can select topics, open subtopics and return to a higher level.
The prompt always shows your current location.

Enter a topic below, for example NAVIGATION.
Press Return on an empty line to move up one level.

///2STARTING
Start HELP without arguments to see the available help files:

  E>HELP

The available .HLP files are listed, followed by the HELP> prompt.
Enter a filename without .HLP, for example TURBOP.
Enter HELP to open this help file about HELP.COM itself.

You can also open a file or topic directly from CP/M:

  E>HELP HELP NAVIGATION
  E>HELP TURBOP COMPILING

With arguments, HELP opens the topic and stays interactive.
For example, HELP PIP continues at HELP>PIP>.
Enter a subtopic name, or press Return to move up one level.

///2NAVIGATION
The prompt shows the selected file and topic path.

  HELP>                  Select a help file.
  HELP>TURBOP>           Select a subtopic of TURBOP.
  HELP>TURBOP>DRIVES>    You are in the DRIVES topic.

Enter a subtopic name without a leading period, then press Return.
Opening a topic displays its text and available subtopics.
Moving up displays the list of topics at the parent level.

No subtopics. means that there are no further subtopics.
An empty line moves up one level. Return at HELP> exits.
Select EXAMPLE, BACK or ABBREVIATION for more information.

///3EXAMPLE
Starting at HELP>, follow these steps:

  Enter TURBOP. The prompt becomes HELP>TURBOP>.
  Enter DRIVES. The prompt becomes HELP>TURBOP>DRIVES>.
  Press Return. HELP>TURBOP> and its subtopic list reappear.
  Enter COMPILING to open that topic.
  Press Return to return to HELP>TURBOP>.
  Enter RUNNING to read the next topic.

A name without a leading period searches under the current topic.
Move up first when you want to select a neighboring topic.

///3BACK
An empty input line moves up exactly one level:

  HELP>TURBOP>DRIVES>  Return  gives  HELP>TURBOP>
  HELP>TURBOP>        Return  gives  HELP>
  HELP>              Return  exits HELP and returns to CP/M.

Moving up does not print the parent topic's text again.
It does list the topics available at the new level.
At the top level, the list of help files appears again.

To switch from TURBOP to MSCOBOL, return to HELP> first.
Then enter MSCOBOL and press Return.

///3ABBREVIATION
Uppercase and lowercase input are treated alike.
You may shorten a topic name if the abbreviation is unique.

At HELP>TURBOP>, COMP can select COMPILING.
The prompt then shows the full name: HELP>TURBOP>COMPILING>.
An exact name takes precedence over a prefix match.

If several names match, HELP reports Ambiguous topic.
Type more letters. Your current path remains unchanged.
An unknown topic also leaves your current location unchanged.

///2PAGING
After twenty displayed lines, HELP shows a paging prompt:

  -- More -- RETURN=continue, Q=quit

Press Return to continue reading.
Q skips the remaining topic text and displays the subtopics.
During a paged subtopic list, Q stops that list.

The two prompts use Return differently:

  At -- More --, Return means: next page.
  At HELP>...>, Return means: move up one level.

Wait for the HELP prompt before entering the next topic.

///2DOTCOMMANDS
A leading period provides a shortcut for relative navigation.
Normal navigation does not require it.

With .NAME, HELP first searches under the current topic.
If nothing matches, it tries a neighboring topic.
This fallback moves up at most one level.

For example, at HELP>TURBOP>COMPILING>, enter .RUNNING.
Because RUNNING is alongside COMPILING, the prompt becomes:

  HELP>TURBOP>RUNNING>

A child topic has priority. An ambiguous name produces an error;
the search does not then continue somewhere else automatically.

///2FILES
HELP reads .HLP files in the current CP/M drive and user area.
The file list at HELP> belongs to that drive and user area.
An explicit drive letter is also accepted, for example:

  E>HELP B:TURBOP

You can also select B:TURBOP at HELP>.
The prompt shows the base filename without its drive or extension.

A help file may contain several top-level topics.
Without a topic name, HELP displays the first top-level topic.
You can explicitly select another one:

  E>HELP CPMHELP DDT

Keep an existing general HELP.HLP as, for example, CPMHELP.HLP
before copying this new HELP.HLP to the same CP/M disk.

///2INDEXED
Original indexed CP/M Plus .HLP files also work.
They do not need to be converted to plain text first.
HELP recognizes the binary index and reads the topic text after it.

Plain-text files and files in this original indexed format can
therefore be used together on the same disk.
This HELP.HLP is a plain-text file without a binary index.

HELP also supports the older HELP.HLP supplied with your system:
it has a missing level on DDT and zero padding at the end.
The supplied HELP.HLP, TURBOP.HLP and MSCOBOL.HLP have been tested.

The .HLP extension alone does not guarantee compatibility.
Other help formats, such as Windows Help, are not supported.

///2MESSAGES
Help file not found.
  The file was not found. Check the name, drive and current
  CP/M user area.

Help topic not found.
  The topic does not exist under the current topic.
  Check the topic list or press Return to move up first.

Ambiguous topic; type more characters.
  Several topics match the abbreviation. Type more letters.

Invalid name or input too long.
  Check the name and input length. See LIMITS for details.

Read error, bad index, or text limit exceeded.
  The file could not be read correctly or exceeds a limit.
  Check the file transfer and HLP format.

///2LIMITS
Filenames use CP/M 8.3 notation with the .HLP extension.
A command may contain up to ten words, including the filename.
Each word may contain up to twelve characters.
A leading period counts towards this limit.

HELP supports topic levels 1 through 9.
A text line may contain up to 255 bytes.
A file may contain up to 65,535 text lines.

CRLF, LF and CR line endings are recognized.
ASCII with CRLF is a suitable format for transfer to CP/M.
This help file uses ASCII, CRLF and trailing Ctrl-Z padding.

///2AUTHOR
Help by Willem Aandewiel
